rate is really low anyway in  2016  only one in 200 phone calls made by a  pollster ended up in a  in an interview which could then go into  the polling sample joe biden got the  most  votes of any presidential candidate in  american history  and donald trump the second most trump's  strategy has always been to play to his  base  but in this election he managed to  broaden it how did he do this  democrats have long argued that high  turnout would benefit democrats the more  people who turned out that would be very  very good for the democrats  actually i think what we've learned is  that high turnout benefits both sides  for me one of the really interesting  lessons of this  this campaign is that the republican  strategy of sort of  white identity politics that's very  closely associated with  with trump you know it may not  necessarily be  the right strategy forward and by by  contrast you know the democrat view that  they will inevitably do well because as  the country is getting more  racially diverse so that necessarily  favors the democrats it's it's not clear  to me that that has to be the case and  that there is therefore an opening there  for a new kind of republicanism that  isn't  trumpism um but that appeals to a  broader more diverse country  i really hope that's true on on the  turnout point  i think it brings us back to where we  began i mean on the one hand as a small  d democrat  it's great to see turn out as high as it  is on the other hand i do think that  high turnout is a function of extreme  partisanship  and the way in which this election felt  like a sort of existential threat  to the future of the country on both  sides and that's why you had so many  um trump voters that's why you had so  many bike voters out so  what would be great for america is you  could if you could keep this high  turnout  without that sense of you know doom  every four years  yeah that's that is absolutely true so  if i'm i've just told you what i feel if  i'm optimistic if i'm feeling  pessimistic then i look at the fact that  both sides were driven  really more by fear and loathing of the  other than anything else  that's what what pushed people out to  vote the senate looks very likely to  remain  in republican control rudy garcia asks  do you think congress will pass any of  biden's proposed legislation  and what would the impact of that be so  assuming it does stay  narrowly in republican hands there is a  view out there that nothing will happen  because you know mitch mcconnell will  you know  block anything that joe biden tries to  do and there'll be the sort of scorched  earth tactics that were used against  president obama  it's possible but i also think there is  some chance  of some cooperation on some aspects  whether it's the stimulus  whether it's infrastructure spending  whether it's even some areas of  climate change policy there'll be much  less than  progressive democrats would like it'll  be much less of an  ambitious transformation but i think  there is a chance for some progress  even with divided government i don't  know about you john are you are you  uh maybe you're a bit more pessimistic  about that than i am i'm a bit more  pessimistic on that than yours i mean  just because  i remember the time you and i were both  based in washington during the obama  presidency  when republicans had a majority in the  senate and it was just a recipe for  gridlock  but maybe i mean i mean you know a  couple of days after the election  there's a feeling of kind of renewal and  optimism i think we've got to start  there  and and hope that they can do it um but  yeah let's see let's see and i think a  lot of it depends  in on where the republican party is  heading  and what the republican party thinks is  its route to victory in 2022 in the  midterms or 2024.  if it thinks it's doubling down on a  kind of you know  new version of trumpism or indeed  trumpism itself then i suspect we're  unlikely to see anything  but if there is a sense that it is  trying to come up with some  somewhat different uh appeal to to a  broader group of people then i think  having  some sense of having worked in a limited  way across the aisle wouldn't  necessarily be a  be a bad calculus for them to make  that's my hope but i'm i am  i'm in determinedly optimistic mood  right now if there is this  gridlock sorry to pull away from your  optimism zany  um one viewer asks whether biden will  have to  govern by executive order to get through  his policy goals  what do you think biden would very much  like to do as much as possible  uh legislatively he's an  institutionalist he's been in the senate  he'd really like to achieve things  through the senate not least because  they're more durable if they're through  the senate but i  think that he won't he won't hold back  from doing things by executive order  if that's the only way to get things  done and certainly there will be quite a  lot of undoing  of president trump's executive orders  and that can be done quickly and then  i'm sure there will be  a lot of very important uh undoing  whether it's in the regulatory area  whether it's on immigration whether  there are sort of some  some totemic trump uh you know actions  which he which president biden would  undo  very very fast i think but beyond that i  think in in sort of really substantive  areas  um whether it's the stimulus whether  it's infrastructure whether it's even  healthcare although i'm even i am not  sure how much can be achieved there  um he would really love to be able to do  something legislatively because that's  the way you actually get durable change  if it's done legislatively we're about  to move on to the international view of  all of this but  just before we do trump has another 73  days as president doug g asks what will  you use this time to do  that is a very very good question and  one could easily  you know get extremely alarmed because  the president of the united states  you know has enormous power i'm actually  not too alarmed i suspect it is going to  be uh  a sort of slightly lonely reality tv  show of a president  fuming brooding furious um  appealing to his base sort of you know  angry about the  the the results saying that it was all  you know unfair and fake  but i wonder whether at some point you  know president trump is is a very savvy  person and he  will be thinking about what is his next  act and is his next bank to run in 2024  and i wouldn't be too surprised if he  sort of announces that is his next act  to become a you know return to being a  major media star  and and as he sort of works at what it  will be don't ever underestimate his  ability  to kind of promote himself market  himself and he'll he'll switch to  whatever he thinks is  you know the most beneficial way to  ensure that he stays the center of  attention for a very long time  there are various things the president  could do through executive order or  executive action particularly in the  area of immigration  also perhaps in foreign policy so we'll  be watching closely over the next few  months to  see if any of that happens but i think  generally the view that he could do all  sorts of awful things on the way out but  you know technically true is probably  overstated there's a limit to what the  president can do  just by fiat thankfully the outside  world has  of course been watching this with baited  breath adam b  asks trump did far better than expected  what signal does this center  other populist movements around the  world well i think that's a very  interesting question  because he did do better than expected  but he didn't win  and in the end you know if you're if you  want to be a successful populist  you've got to win and so i'm i'm not so  sure that this is given  huge sucker i think it has broadly  reinforced the notion  that anti-immigration sort of  populist strongman-ishi kind of tactics  have a large amount of appeal but in the  us at least they didn't have quite  enough appeal to keep him in the white  house  i was based in brazil for three years  for the economist and so on election  night  i was texting with a brazilian friend  who was watching the results coming in  and assumed that my brazilian friend  assumed there was a kind of one-to-one  correlation between how well trump did  and how well bolsonaro  would do in brazil's next election which  just was a reminder for me how many  people around the world  look at american politics and read into  it something about  politics in in their own country i i'd  agree with zany i mean  trump lost and so you know that cast  some doubt on the future of the kind of  nationalist  you know illiberal populism that's been  sweeping the world over the past few  years  but it was not a it was a  defeat but not a repudiation i think so  i think there's enough in there  for those sorts of figures like you know  bolsonaro duterte  and marine le pen to some extent to look  and say well actually you know there's a  lot of people who  who want this stuff and donald trump's  defeat  i think doesn't change that and how  might a biden presidency change the  relationship between  america and china that question's from  elmer deng  i think that's a really really important  question possibly the single biggest  foreign policy question  um and i my answer would be that i think  the rhetoric will change  there'll be more talk of human rights  there'll be more of a sort of  calm approach from the u.s rather than  sort of  overnight sanctions that no one was  expecting but broadly  and this is really important broadly the  position of the u.s will not change  i don't expect any sudden ending of the  tariff war and diminution of tariffs  i don't expect any kind of softer  approach to china  for the simple reason that there is now  a bipartisan consensus  in the us that china is a  strategic competitor that doesn't play  by the rules that is increasingly  aggressive  that needs to be checked and i think  that's felt on both sides of the aisle  one area that i think president trump  history will say that he deserved credit  is that he kind of shook the world to  realizing that that there really it  hadn't been realized so much before  and he really kind of got that on the  agenda and he shook the perception of  china  now what's needed is a you know calmer  more strategic way of thinking about how  do you actually then work with this  second biggest economy  this rising power of the 21st century  which is has a completely different  non-democratic system it's an  authoritarian country that's  increasingly assertive  how do you deal with it and i think that  the hopeful  view is that the foreign policy  establishment types who will be  part of the biden administration will  will kind of take the world as it is now  shaken up by donald trump and apply some  more strategy to it and ideally  work with other allies work with the  europeans work with other allies in asia  to have a kind of united  front um with which to sort of deal with  china  i was talking to david rennie who's the  economist beijing bureau chief about  this the holiday and he  he was somebody who was in washington  with with danny and with me when we were  based there  and he said something interesting which  was that  the experience of trump in beijing and  for the chinese communist party  basically convinced all the officials  there that whatever  american rhetoric might be under biden  basically  america is in the business now of  stopping china's rise trying to prevent  it by any means kind of  fair or foul and i think that's  interesting because you know four years  of donald trump and then donald trump  getting the second highest  vote total in american history i think  is a reminder to  countries abroad that you know he may  have lost but this strain in america is  not going away  and so you know any future deals that  might be made by  biden administration are pretty  contingent really because  the more isolationist streak in american  foreign policy is never  that far away from from winning and i  think that will affect  american foreign policy going forward as  well that's really interesting  and leads very nicely onto the last  question we'll take from our viewers  paramount a wants to know will the  republican party continue with trump's  ideology and methods  into the future so essentially what  happens to trumpism after trump  it's a question our lexington column  grapples with which you can read by  clicking on the link above  danny what do you think so i think  that's that's you know one of the single  biggest questions hanging over american  politics  and um i i would answer it the following  way i think there are sort of  two elements to trumpism if you will  there is the element that has to do with  the personality  of donald trump himself the  unpredictability the divisive rhetoric  the kind of you know contempt for  institutions the  the sort of nasty approach if you will  to to the conduct of government  and then there is the the policy element  which is kind of  anti-immigration protectionist  uh very much sort of you know america  first-ish  and i suspect that the republican party  is  actually going to stick with a lot of  those strategic elements i think the  republican party  you know the party of reagan reaganism  was all about deregulation and tax cuts  and the shining city on the hill and you  know a very sort of  um strong and expansive foreign policy  the current  republican party is focused you know  it's a it's a party of  of the kind of white working class if  you will it's a party of america  firstism it's a protectionist party  it's a party that's deeply skeptical of  immigration and i think that's going to  stay  i i don't know about you john but i  think that we may have someone whether  it's  you know senator tom cotton whether it's  you know senator marco rubio there are  there are many people who  have broadly the same views as donald  trump but are a sort of  you know cleaned up version of it uh and  will  so you you'll have the kind of bits of  trumpism without trump  i agree 100 with that it's going to be  one of the big questions  that we're covering over the next few  months and few years really i mean right  until dara i mentioned it the next  presidential  primary my assumption like you zany is  that the republican party is the party  of the white working class  for the next few cycles at least and the  republican party has  struggled for a while to come up with a  successor to the reagan formula the  deregulation and tax cuts  which was a highly successful formula  but at a time when  personal tax rates were really high in  america and the economy was more heavily  regulated and sort of once you've done  reaganism once you know so what's the  encore and  trumpism um zami described it is sort of  the republican party's  you know best attempt at a sequel i  think to reaganism  and i don't see them ditching it quickly  that said you know just to echo what  zany says  it's unclear how trumpism without trump  works  i don't know what the trump movement  would look like without him  at the head of it we also don't quite  know how he's going  to behave over the next you know two  three four years you know will he keep  teasing the republican party  with the threat of running again in the  primary in 2024  and in which case that will make it  quite hard for the party to  to to move on from him in any meaningful  way one thing i'm really fairly sure  about is that donald trump is not going  to kind of  quietly go off to a golf course and and  sort of fade from the headlines he's  you know he he has his entire career he  craves more than anything being the  center of attention and he's not going  to suddenly stop wanting to do that  and therefore what better way to ensure  that you are continually the center of  attention than to  at least tease the idea that you will  run again in 2024 or if not you one of  your children  and and the sort of trump brand needs  that expectation because otherwise you  know  the whole kind of the trump brand loses  its luster for many many  you know in it for many parts of america  you know it'll become irrelevant and  irrelevance is something  i think that is worrying for donald  trump more than almost anything else  he absolutely will do anything not to be  irrelevant and looking ahead what's the  economist going to be focusing on  in the coming days well i hope john's  going to get some time off he's been  working unbelievably hard john and his  colleagues have done a fantastic job  laying out how we got here and  and i can say that john's too modest to  to do so i think he's done he and his  colleagues have done a really fantastic  job at doing that and now the next step  is to help  our readers and viewers understand where  america is going to go next and i  suspect that means more focus on  you know what will abide administration  look like what kind of country will it  have what should the world expect  fantastic well that seems like a good  place to end thank you danny thank you  john  thank you thank you i'm zany minton  bedos the economist editor-in-chief  all of our election coverage is up on  economist.com  read it by clicking the link and don't  forget to subscribe  thanks for watching
 

